{"name":"Duck Hunt","hex":"#005800","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"duck","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To quickly lower (the head or body), often in order to prevent it from being struck by something.","To lower (something) into water; to thrust or plunge under liquid and suddenly withdraw."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cave passage containing water with low, or no, airspace."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/duck"},{"term":"hunt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To find or search for an animal in the wild with the intention of killing the animal for its meat or for sport.","To try to find something; search (for)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of hunting.","A hunting expedition."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hunt"}],"entries":[{"title":"Duck Hunt","blocks":[{"text":"Duck Hunt is a 1984 light gun shooter video game developed and published by Nintendo for the Nintendo Entertainment System (NES). The game was first released in April 1984 in Japan for the Family Computer (Famicom) console and in North America as an arcade game for the Nintendo VS. System. It became a launch game for the NES in North America in October 1985, and was re-released in Europe two years later. Players fire the NES Zapper at a CRT television, with three attempts per round to shoot ducks and clay pigeons."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/14/DuckHuntBox.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck_Hunt","wikidata":"Q764069"},{"title":"Duck","blocks":[{"text":"Duck is the common name for numerous species of waterfowl in the family Anatidae. Ducks are generally smaller and shorter-necked than swans and geese, which are members of the same family. Divided among several subfamilies, they are a form taxon; they do not represent a monophyletic group, since swans and geese are not considered ducks. Ducks are mostly aquatic birds, and may be found in both fresh water and sea water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg/330px-Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck","wikidata":"Q3736439"},{"title":"Hunting","blocks":[{"text":"Hunting is the human practice of seeking, pursuing, capturing, and/or killing wildlife or feral animals. The most common reasons for humans to hunt are to obtain the animal's body to consume their meat and useful animal products, for recreation/taxidermy, although it may also be done for other purposes such as removing predators dangerous to humans or domestic animals, to eliminate pests and nuisance animals that damage crops/livestock/poultry or spread diseases, for trade/tourism, or for ecological conservation against overpopulation and invasive species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Paul_Childerley_driven_hunt_Finland_04.png/330px-Paul_Childerley_driven_hunt_Finland_04.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunting","wikidata":"Q36963"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d8/Duck_hunt_pic.PNG/250px-Duck_hunt_pic.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d8/Duck_hunt_pic.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d8/Duck_hunt_pic.PNG/250px-Duck_hunt_pic.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADuck_hunt_pic.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/22/Duck_Hunt_%28NES%29_clay_pigeon_mode.png/250px-Duck_Hunt_%28NES%29_clay_pigeon_mode.png?utm_source=en.wi